  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nDangerous heat will impact the region over the weekend and\npotentially extending into early next week. The peak strength of\nthe upper level ridge is forecast to occur on Friday and Saturday\nbefore weakening Sunday into Monday. Even though guidance is\nforecasting record H5 heights on Friday and Saturday, forecast \nhighs are barely reaching daily records. This is either due to the\nmodest low level moisture remaining in place holding down daytime\ntemperatures a bit, or the NBM is under forecasting temperatures.\nWe are leaning toward the NBM deterministic forecast temperatures\nnot being hot enough, but not enough to warrant nudging the \nforecast upward. It would not be surprising to have actual highs \ncloser to the 75-90th percentile of guidance, or a couple degrees \nhigher than the current forecast. We are likely to have some areas\nreaching 120 degrees across the lower deserts of southeast \nCalifornia on Saturday and Sunday, while the Phoenix area could \nconceivably reach 118 or even 119 degrees. Overnight lows will \nalso provide little relief from the extreme heat with readings \nmostly in the upper 80s to the lower 90s to as high as the mid 90s\nin the urban core of the Phoenix metro.\n\nMonsoon activity is expected to remain very limited on Saturday\nwith little to no shot at any outflows nearing the lower deserts,\nbut that may change by Sunday or Monday. As the ridge begins to\nweaken Sunday into Monday, guidance shows evidence of increasing\nlow level moisture and more monsoon activity across eastern\nArizona and northern Mexico. However, chances still remain fairly\nlow on Sunday and Monday with high terrain PoPs of 20-30% and \n10-15% into the Phoenix area during the evening hours. As long as \nthe ridge continues to weaken early next week, monsoon convection \nshould also increase, especially by the middle of next week as \nthere are hints of a potential easterly wave moving into or near \nArizona. As the ridge weakens and moisture increases, temperatures\nare likely to drift lower, but another day or two of Major to \nExtreme HeatRisk looks possible for Monday and/or Tuesday, \nespecially across southeast California and southwest Arizona.",
